@aojea and all others, Thank you very much for investigating the issue and proposing solutions.
As the OpenStack provider does not support HTTP health checks for services with an externalTrafficPolicy other than Local (and even then only for checking the "healthCheckNodePort"), a solution must be implemented at the provider level.
I completely understand why the NotReady condition itself is not an effective indicator for removing a node from load balancing, and I believe this issue can be closed for the SIG Network.

I think we still need an answer on these questions: https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/issues/131026#issuecomment-2755193488

@TP-O Thanks for the contribution, but https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/pull/133497 looks like it's trying to work around the throttling issue by increasing the dimensionality of the events. Discussed in the sig node CI meeting that we don't want to go in that direction, and if it's a throttling issue we should accept that. 

If it is throttling we can close this issue, but if it's something else we can take a closer look.

I'm not sure I can reproduce the problem, take a look at this:
```go
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"math"
)

func main() {
	// usageRatio := float64(usage) / float64(targetUsage)
	// https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/blob/7b80cdb66a390f225d23cd612950144e3a39d1ae/pkg/controller/podautoscaler/replica_calculator.go#L260
	below := float64(214748364) / float64(0.1)
	above := float64(214748365) / float64(0.1)

	// replicaCount = int32(math.Ceil(usageRatio * float64(readyPodCount)))
	// https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/blob/7b80cdb66a390f225d23cd612950144e3a39d1ae/pkg/controller/podautoscaler/replica_calculator.go#L278
	fmt.Println(int32(math.Ceil(below * float64(int64(100)))))
	fmt.Println(int32(math.Ceil(above * float64(int64(100)))))
}
```

```console
$ go run .
2147483647
2147483647
```

Great to see #127050 was merged! Thanks for everyone involved.
@omerap12 Are you planning to go forward with the other cases in this file like you mentioned [here](https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/pull/126979#issuecomment-2320328068)?
To be honest, I'm not sure which of these unsafe casts are the cause for the original bug, so I don't know if this issue is resolved or not.

@pbetkier Not sure whether autoscaler detects all unschedulable pods correctly in https://github.com/kubernetes/autoscaler/issues/7343, but I think that the problem might still exist even if it wasn't an issue.

Not sure how the simulation works, but is it possible that it can give different results than the real scheduler and cause autoscaler to take wrong actions? Let's consider a scenario that there are 10 nodes to turn down and 1 Pod still waiting for PVC provisioning. How autoscaler knows which one to keep?

Communicating scheduler decisions for all kinds of delayed binding scenarios (preemption, PVC or DRA) should be beneficial. Reusing the `status.nominatedNodeName` for that seems to be a good idea.

I'm tempted to say we should just deprecate DELETECOLLECTION and make clients implement it. It is literally faster to use curl and bash to delete everything on a large collection right now.

A client can do this super fast:

```
for collectionNonEmpty() {
  for p := nextListPage() {
    for item := range p.Items {
      if item.Metadata.DeletionTimestamp == "" {
        go deleteItem(item.Metadata.Namespace, item.Metadata.Name)
      }
    }
  }
}
```

Pre-pages, clients could not do this super fast because the list smashed the server.

So, now that we have pagination, I think it might make sense to move delete collection back into the client library.

@hgokavarapuz Are you sure that this actually fixes the problem and doesn't just require more image downloads for the bug to occur?

In my case this was happening within the GKE allowed disk sizes, so I'd say there's definitely still some sort of bug in GKE here at least. 

It would also be good to have some sort of official position on the minimum disk size required in order to run kubernetes on a node without getting this error. Otherwise it's not clear exactly how large the volumes must be in order to be within spec for running kubernetes.
